<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uganda Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Uganda Railway is a metre-gauge railway built by the British from 1896 to 1901 from Mombasa on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> to Kisumu on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>. Built largely by labourers recruited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, some 2,500 of whom died, it opened the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> to trade and settlement, led to the founding of Nairobi and became the backbone of the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uganda-railway.jpg" alt="Uganda Railway" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rod Waddington from Kergunyah, Australia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uganda_Railway_Poster_(15069546274).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the railway the Imperial British East Africa Company had begun the Mackinnon-Sclater road, a 970-kilometre ox-cart track from Mombasa to Busia, in 1890. In December 1890, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had joined the anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> measures of the Brussels Conference Act, the Foreign Office proposed a railway to Uganda to disrupt the slave traffic from the interior to the coast; it would also let Britain move people and soldiers into the African Great Lakes region. Captain James Macdonald surveyed the route from December 1891 to November 1892.</p><p>The Uganda Railway Act 1896 authorised the line, which George Whitehouse built as chief engineer. It was named after its destination, though its whole original length of 1,060 kilometres lay in what would become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>. Rails, sleepers and girders were imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, which required a modern port at Kilindini Harbour in Mombasa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>More than 30,000 labourers were contracted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, most from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. Historians describe harsh conditions, with disease, accidents and wild animals taking a heavy toll; in 1898 a pair of lions at the Tsavo River killed at least 28 workers. Europeans held the senior posts, Indians worked as clerks, artisans and supervisors, and Africans were forced into the hardest tasks. Many Indian workers stayed on, founding a permanent Indian community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. The Nandi resisted the line under Koitalel Arap Samoei until he was killed in 1905.</p><p>In Parliament Henry Labouchère called it a lunatic line. Ferries shipped in parts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> ran from Kisumu across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>, and branch lines followed from 1913. In 1929 the railway became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> and Uganda Railways and Harbours, and in 1948 part of the East African Railways Corporation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The railway carried travellers to the safaris of the early 20th century, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a> in 1909. After independence it fell into disrepair; the last metre-gauge train between Mombasa and Nairobi ran on 28 April 2017, and the Mombasa–Nairobi Standard Gauge Railway, built alongside it from 2014 to 2016, opened to passengers on 31 May 2017. Charles Miller's book of 1971 gave the line its later name, the Lunatic Express.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uganda Railway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the headquarters of Portuguese power in East Africa, its capture by Omani forces in 1698 after a long siege ended Portuguese rule north of the Ruvuma, and the town later became the base of the Mazrui governors and, under British rule, the starting point of the <b>Uganda Railway</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The first settlers arrived in 1902 as part of Governor Charles Eliot's plan to have a settler economy pay for the <b>Uganda Railway</b>, and over the next three decades the colonial government and settlers consolidated their control over Kenyan land and forced native Kenyans to become wage labourers.</blockquote><a href="/movement/mau-mau-rebellion">Mau Mau rebellion · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Uganda Railway</b>, built from 1896 to 1901 largely by Indian indentured workers, opened the interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/east-africa-protectorate">East Africa Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Uganda Railway</b> reached Lake Victoria in 1901, and African smallholders grew cotton and later coffee, with Indian traders handling much of the commerce.</blockquote><a href="/state/uganda-protectorate">Uganda Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
